# Admin

`/admin` sits outside the wallet layout. `openAdmin()` in `lib/admin.server.ts` reads the Better Auth session and allows the visit only when that Google email is in the `ADMIN_EMAILS` env var (comma-separated). Comparison ignores case and surrounding spaces. Read it as `process.env.ADMIN_EMAILS`. An empty value lets nobody in. A signed-out visit shows the sign-in screen and returns to `/admin`. A signed-in account that is not on the list gets the missing page.

Do not import `lib/admin.server.ts` from a client component. The account menu learns about access through `AdminAccess`, a boolean passed from the server. The email list stays on the server.

The page reads counts from `profile`, `wait_alert`, `push_subscription`, and `schedule`, plus the cached CBP snapshot. It also reads one row set per table (`profile`, `streak`, `preference`, `wait_alert`, `schedule`, `push_subscription`, plus `usage_event` via `readUsageTotals()`) and joins them in memory by account id in `buildInsights()` (`lib/admin-insights.ts`, client-safe, tested in `tests/admin-insights.test.ts`). A missing table counts as empty. That one model feeds every section. The stats row (one grouped surface) sits above the tabs and is always visible, and the CBP snapshot age sits in the page header; everything else sits in one of five tabs in `AdminTabs` (`components/admin/admin-tabs.tsx`, client, a sliding-pill segmented control with a critically damped cross-fade between panels - no overshoot, since switching tabs is a selection, not a flick). The bar is sticky and translucent (solid under `prefers-reduced-transparency`), and arrow keys, Home, and End move between tabs. The tabs are grouped by the question each answers: Overview (Arrivals then Journey, the funnel from first visit to asking, with the active/idle counts in its hint, and Growth), Acquisition (Where they came from, Invites, and Campaigns), Accounts (`AccountList`, full width), Usage (Crossings: who saved/alerted/scheduled each port beside its current wait, Feature use, Exchange, and Opened often), and Cameras (Nuevo and Viejo from `camera_stream`, merged with `listedCameras()`, and every group in `FEED_GROUPS` from `camera_feed`). `AdminDashboard` stays a server component; it builds each tab's JSX (including client children like `AccountList`) and hands it to `AdminTabs` as a named prop, so the tab-switching state lives in one small client component instead of the whole page. `AccountList` searches the loaded accounts by name or email. Every row shows Location: the latitude and longitude they shared, or a dash when that account has none. Opening the account repeats it as a link that opens the point on a map. Filter narrows that same list in the browser, and the choices combine: active in the last 7 days or idle, how they found the app, language, whether they brought someone in or arrived from an invite, and a journey step they reached. Each option shows how many accounts it would leave, given the search and the other choices. Following an invite link clears the search and the filter so that account stays on screen. The list shows 25 at a time with "Show more", sorts by last active, newest, or most engaged (`sortedAccounts`), and shows times as "3h ago" with the exact Central time in the tooltip. All admin times come from `lib/admin-time.ts` and carry the zone. Growth (`GrowthChart`, client) receives 90 Central days of new accounts and of new guests. A guest is a `visitor` row that still has no account, counted on the day of `first_seen`; a browser that later signs in leaves that series and counts as an account on the day the profile was created. Accounts are the dark segment and guests the accent one, stacked in one bar per day. The chart lets the viewer pick 7, 30, or 90, compare the combined total to the period before, and press or drag across bars to read one day with both running totals. `readGuestDays` loads those counts in SQL, and `applyGuestGrowth` lays them onto the account days. Day labels are calendar days, never shifted by a zone. Crossings, Invites, and Sources use `MoreRows` (first 8, then all). A missing camera table still lists the address from code, marked built in. A row marks that camera saved and shows the stored address. `ACCOUNT_LIMIT` (10,000) is only a safety ceiling, because the funnel, sources, growth, and crossings are all built from those rows; do not lower it to shrink the list, page the list instead. The page does not write those tables, except `ensureProfile` for the admin on that visit. It does not show push endpoints.

## Campaigns

`campaign` is one row per link an admin created: `slug`, `name`, `created_at`. The slug is the name folded into a short code (`instagram-october`). `POST /api/admin/campaigns` re-checks the session and `isAdminEmail`, the same way announcements do, and refuses a code that already exists. The Accounts tab (`Campaigns`) lists each one with how many new accounts signed in from it (`profile.campaign`) and how many browsers opened the link (`visitor.campaign`, one row per browser). Counts are read in SQL, so they are not cut off by `ACCOUNT_LIMIT`. The people in `ANALYTICS_EXCLUDED_EMAILS` are left out of the account count. A code that is not a campaign is not stored. The link is `/?c=<slug>`. Opening an account that arrived from one shows the campaign's name. The page render still does not write `campaign`; creating one is the POST.

## Saved by city

The Usage tab's Saved by city (`savedByCity` in `admin.server.ts`) groups every account's saved bridges (`preference.wallet`) by the city `portName` files them under, and by border (Mexican or Canadian, from the feed's `border`). `accounts` are distinct people per row, and one person with bridges in two cities counts in both, so rows add up to more than the account total. Bridges whose port the feed no longer lists are counted as `unplaced`. This is where riders keep bridges, not where they live; `profile.latitude` and `longitude` exist only for people who shared a location. `ANALYTICS_EXCLUDED_EMAILS` are left out.

## Arrivals and crossing opens

`lib/analytics.ts` (client-safe) holds `ANALYTICS_EXCLUDED_EMAILS` (`orlandovaluta568@gmail.com`, compared ignoring case), `VISITOR_COOKIE`, and `OFTEN_OPENS` (3). That address is left out of every figure: `buildInsights` drops that profile and its rows from every table first, the stat counts in `admin.server.ts` filter it in SQL, `POST /api/views` writes nothing for it, and `ensureProfile` deletes its `visitor` row instead of linking it. Stored rows stay in the database and are only skipped when the page is built.

`visitor` (`lib/visitor.server.ts`) is one row per browser that painted the sign-in screen: `id`, `first_seen`, `last_seen`, nullable `user_id` that cascades from `profile`. `SignInScreen` posts once per page load to `POST /api/visitor`, which sets the httpOnly `usbordertime.visitor` cookie (one year, a random UUID) and upserts the row. `ensureProfile` runs `claimVisitor` after the profile exists and sets `user_id` only when it is still empty, so a shared computer stays with its first account. Clearing cookies and returning signed out is a new arrival, and one person on two devices can be one conversion and one leftover arrival. The Overview tab's Arrivals reads counts in SQL (`readArrivals`): all time and the last 7 days, how many signed in and how many did not.

## Uses

`open` is a `usage_event` kind: one row each time a signed-in account comes into the app. A guest open is a `guest_event` row (`kind` `open`) on the visitor id, and a guest look at a crossing is `kind` `view` with the port number. Uses adds those opens to Times opened (blue segment) and counts the browsers as Unique guests. Each crossing row adds how many guests opened it. `OpenSync`, mounted from `SessionGate`, posts `POST /api/opens` on load, and again when the app returns to the front after being away for 30 seconds. A repeat for that account within 15 seconds is not stored, and a reload in the same tab within 2 minutes does not post again, so finishing the welcome questions stays one use. `ANALYTICS_EXCLUDED_EMAILS` writes nothing.

The Overview tab's Uses starts on the last 7 Central days and lets the viewer pick Today, 7, 30, or 90, or type a span up to a year (`GET /api/admin/opens?from=&to=`, both `YYYY-MM-DD`, inclusive, read in `America/Chicago`). Unique accounts is distinct accounts across that whole span, not the sum of the days, and Unique guests is the same for browsers with no account. Opens is every entry, accounts and guests together. Pressing or dragging the bars reads one day. A `visit` is the older once-a-day record of coming in, so those rows count too. A visit on a day that already has an `open` for that account is left out. Feature use still calls `visit` Days. An `open` still counts toward last active.

`port_view` (`lib/port-view.server.ts`) is one row per `(user_id, port_number)` with `opens` and `last_opened_at`, cascading from `profile`. `trackPortView` (`lib/port-view.ts`) posts when `WalletApp`'s open card changes and when a Browse row opens; the stack does not count. `POST /api/views` needs a session and an `isPortNumber` value, and ignores a repeat for the same account and port within 10 seconds. `buildInsights` turns the rows into per-crossing `opens` and `viewers`, per-account `opens` and `topBridges`, and `often`: accounts with at least `OFTEN_OPENS` opens of a crossing they have no alert or alarm on. That list only informs; nothing is sent from it.

## Notify everyone

"Notify everyone" is one push, sent right away to every row in `push_subscription`, whatever account it belongs to - for an app change worth telling people about, not a wait or a schedule (those stay in `alerts.mdc`). `lib/announcement.ts` is client-safe: it holds `Announcement`, the length limits, and `isAnnouncementUrl`, which only accepts a same-origin path (`/...`), the same rule every other notification link in this app follows. `lib/announcement.server.ts` is not: `sendAnnouncement()` reads every device, posts the same `{ title, body, url, tag }` payload `sendPush` already understands (`public/sw.js` needed no changes), forgets any device that answers gone, and writes one `announcement` row with how many accepted it. `ensureTable()` runs `ensureTables()` from `alert.server.ts` first, since it reads `push_subscription`. A title or body that's empty after trimming does not send. No `push_subscription` rows at all answers "no devices" instead of writing a row for zero people.

`GET /api/admin/announcements` and `POST /api/admin/announcements` both re-check the session and `isAdminEmail` themselves - they do not call `openAdmin()`, since that also builds the whole dashboard. `AdminDashboard` reads the latest rows once, from `admin.server.ts`'s own `listAnnouncements()`, and passes them to `AnnouncementModal` (header button, "Notify" with a megaphone) as `initial`. The modal holds its own open state and portals a dialog - same shape as `ScheduleSheet` (bottom sheet on a phone, centered on `sm:` and up) - around `AnnouncementComposer`; sending updates the history list inside it without a reload or closing it. The id is `randomUUID()` from `node:crypto`, stored as `text`, the same shape `schedule.id` uses. On a desktop width the dialog widens (`sm:max-w-3xl`) and splits into the composer and the history side by side.

Each announcement has `active` (`active boolean not null default true`, added with `add column if not exists`; when the column is first added, every row but the newest is turned off). At most one is on: `sendAnnouncement` turns every older row off after the insert, and `setAnnouncementActive()` turns the chosen one on and all others off in one `update`, so an older broadcast comes back by switching the newest off and the older on. Off keeps the history row and does not recall the push. `readLatestAnnouncement` reads the row `where active`, newest first, so riders see that one or nothing. The history list has a switch per row, backed by `PATCH /api/admin/announcements` (`{ id, active }`, admin re-checked like the other verbs); `setAnnouncementActive` expires the `latest-announcement` tag like `sendAnnouncement` does. The switch moves first and goes back if the request fails. A rider who already dismissed or is looking at one is not affected until their next load.

In development, the account menu has "Preview announcement" (`DevAnnouncementPreview`). It opens `AnnouncementNotice` with a stand-in message. Dismissing that preview does not write `usbordertime.announcement.seen.v1`. The button is not rendered outside development.

The composer chooses `surface`: `banner` or `modal`. That is stored on the `announcement` row (`surface text not null default 'modal'`, added with `add column if not exists` so a table created before the choice still accepts it). A missing or unknown value is read as `modal`. The push payload does not include it.

The push is not the only place a broadcast lands. `latestAnnouncement()` in `lib/announcement.server.ts` reads the single newest row through `unstable_cache` (tag `latest-announcement`, one hour at most), so a page load does not open the database for it. `sendAnnouncement` is the only writer and expires that tag right after the insert; any new write to `announcement` must do the same. `AppLayout`'s `LiveWallet` fetches it alongside the CBP snapshot and the camera playlist, so every signed-in rider sees it next time they open the app - not only the devices that had push permission on when "Notify everyone" was pressed. It waits until this browser has finished or skipped the welcome tour, and it stays hidden while that tour is on screen, including a replay. `modal` is `AnnouncementNotice` (`components/wallet/announcement-notice.tsx`): a sheet on a phone, centered from `sm` up. A body that contains `•` is drawn as a list; anything else stays one paragraph. Closing it — the X, the backdrop, or Escape — remembers that announcement's id in `lib/announcement-store.ts` (`usbordertime.announcement.seen.v1`), so it does not come back on this device. Escape is captured and marked handled so a card behind the sheet does not also close. `banner` is `AnnouncementBanner`, under the bridges header, hidden while a card is open or a notification's bridges are on screen. View or Dismiss remembers the same id. A later broadcast has a new id and shows again either way. View also navigates to the announcement's `url`, the same same-origin path a push would open.
